 2. Conference crystal ball 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 An F-22 ascends over Washington in July. Photo: Colin Demarest/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Air, Space &amp; Cyber Conference kicks off in two days.

 Why it matters: It means another pilgrimage to National Harbor, Maryland, for thousands of us. The world's largest defense contractors — and the hottest upstarts — will soon mingle with civilian and uniformed military leadership .

 Here are four predictions for Sept. 14–16. Mileage may vary.

 The show will be dominated by hardware. Yes, networks and cybersecurity matter, but the Air Force is now in pursuit of new fighters, bombers and drones, including the F-47 (Boeing), the B-21 (Northrop Grumman), the FQ-42 (General Atomics), the FQ-44 (Anduril Industries) and Massed Modular Aircraft (to be determined). The Space Force will further loosen its collar when it comes to using words like "weapon," driven in part by the Trump administration's Golden Dome . For years such phrasing was taboo. Times have changed, and so have the threats. The Iran war will color every discussion. Warplanes have been destroyed; bases and radars bombed. American troops are dead. Propulsion will be the dark horse. Someone's got to power these things. More from Axios: 

